## Architectural Comparison

| Feature | Monolithic | Three-Tier | Microservices |
|---|---|---|---|
| **Code Organisation** | Single project & repo | Split by tier | Many independent services |
| **Deployment** | One server for everything | Separate servers per tier | Distributed across many servers |
| **Scalability** | Hard to scale parts independently | Can scale tiers separately | Each service scales independently |
| **Complexity** | Low ops, can get messy in code | Moderate, clear structure | High ops complexity |
| **Best For** | Small/early-stage projects | Intermediate apps | Large, complex systems |

---

## Key Takeaways

- **MVC** is a design pattern — it organises code, not infrastructure.
- **Three-tier architecture** is a deployment model — it separates frontend, backend, and database onto distinct servers.
- The line between monolith and microservices is a **spectrum** — avoid over-engineering early.
- Scaling challenges (load balancing, caching, multiple servers) are addressed as the system grows.
- Modern best practice: **dedicated frontend** (React etc.) rather than server-rendered UI.
- Cloud knowledge (AWS etc.) is essential — most production systems live in the cloud.
